Prompt 1


You are dispatched to the scene of a motor vehicle collision. As you are en route, dispatch alerts you that there are multiple vehicles involved. You know that it is dark out and the snow has been coming down since early the afternoon. Additionally, the location that dispatch gives you is one of the major highways that runs through your town. 

As part of your preparation, you are imagining what the scene may look like. Just from the information given to you by dispatch, list 3 scene safety hazards that you may encounter.   

 

Three significant hazards I would anticipate are poor visibility, hazardous road conditions, and secondary collisions.

First, darkness and heavy snowfall would severely reduce visibility, making it difficult for approaching drivers to see the accident scene, emergency vehicles, and first responders. Second, accumulated snow and ice could make the highway slippery, increasing the risk of vehicles losing control and emergency personnel slipping or falling. Third, because the collision involves multiple vehicles on a major highway, there is a high risk of additional collisions from approaching traffic. I would also remain alert for leaking fuel, fire, unstable vehicles, and downed electrical wires. I would request law enforcement and fire department assistance to secure the highway before entering the scene.


  

Rubric
 There are a number of things that can make a scene hazardous, from the location, other hazards, weather, and even patients. There are likely many variations of these that your colleagues may identify from the prompt above, so try and picture what the scene may look like, and see if they hazard listed would fit from the information given. The particular ones that may stand out are location, time of day, multiple vehicles, weather, and the nature of the accident. These may be combined or arranged in a variety of ways, for example, someone may think that because it is dark and on a highway, that can be one or two separate hazards.  

