The time has come to transport your patient, and you need to direct your patient where to go. Your patient is repeatedly asking to go to the small community hospital, but you believe that she would be better served somewhere else. You have completed your secondary survey, and found a hematoma to the back of her scalp, an obvious deformity to her right arm, and multiple abrasions across her chest and abdomen. She continues to perseverate and ask you the same questions over and over. Her vital signs are as follows: 

Blood pressure 88/50

Heart rate 96

Respiratory rate 36

 What is the most appropriately type of hospital to transport this patient to, and discuss the reasons why.  

If I were caring for this patient, I would choose a hospital with a high-level trauma service, preferably a Level I trauma center, because her injuries appear too serious for a small community hospital to manage safely.

The biggest warning sign for me is her blood pressure of 88/50, which makes me concerned that she could be losing blood internally. Her breathing rate of 36 is also much faster than normal. This tells me that her body is under significant stress and that she may have an injury affecting her breathing. I would also be worried about her head because she has swelling behind her scalp and keeps asking the same questions. This behavior could mean she has suffered a brain injury. Her injured arm and the marks across her chest and stomach make me concerned about additional damage that I cannot see during my examination.

Even though she wants to go to her local hospital, I would explain that her condition requires a facility equipped to handle major injuries. A trauma center would have specialists who could quickly evaluate her head, chest, and abdomen and provide emergency surgery if necessary. I would also consider whether her confusion affects her ability to make medical decisions and follow EMS protocols for transport. I believe taking her directly to an appropriate trauma center would give her the best opportunity to receive the care she needs without unnecessary delays.

Rubric
 For a number of reasons, including injuries, mechanism, and vital signs, this patient needs to be transport to a trauma center. Any combination of these things can be used as justification, as long as they fit with the patient that is presented. Particularly, her perseveration indications a possible head injury, she has an elevated respiratory rate, and was involved in an significant motor vehicle collision. She also has abrasions that could indicate internal bleeding, and obvious orthopedic injuries. Another acceptable description would be a trauma patient with obvious multisystems trauma.  

