Prompt 7
As you are getting ready to leave the scene, someone opens the back door of your ambulance, and you recognize it to be one of the firefighters from the scene. They ask if you can take 2 additional patients, both green, with you. You agree, and the climb into the back of your ambulance. You continue to care for the red patient on the cot as you drive emergently. The other two patients are crying and overwhelmed.

As you continue to care for your patients, your partner alerts you that you are approximately 5 minutes from your destination. You pick up your phone to call in your arrival.

Write an example of the call you may make, including information that you feel is relevant to include.

You will be graded on the inclusion of four essential pieces of information in your call, although you may have more than that. You will not lose points for having additional information, as long as it is not completely extraneous.

For example, if these patients were from an anthrax exposure, you would want to include how many patients there are. However, you would not want to include the fact that one patient liked the color green when they were a child. Don’t include ALL the information because you do not want to lose points.

"Trauma Center, this is Ambulance 4, inbound from a multiple-vehicle mass casualty incident on the highway. We are approximately five minutes from your facility.

We are transporting three patients: one critical red-tagged trauma patient and two stable green-tagged patients.

Our critical patient is a 24-year-old female driver involved in a high-impact motor vehicle collision. She is confused, repeatedly asking the same questions, and experiencing significant respiratory distress. Assessment reveals a posterior scalp hematoma, an obvious right arm deformity, and multiple abrasions across her chest and abdomen. We are concerned about traumatic brain injury, internal hemorrhage, and shock.

Her latest vital signs are blood pressure 88/50, heart rate 96, and respiratory rate 36. We have maintained spinal motion restriction as indicated, completed our primary assessment, and are continuing supportive care and reassessment during transport.

We request immediate trauma team activation and evaluation upon arrival. Our two additional patients are ambulatory, classified green, and currently stable, although emotionally distressed. ETA five minutes. Do you have any questions or additional instructions?"

Four essential elements included
1. Identification and estimated arrival time: Ambulance identification, incoming trauma alert, and five-minute ETA.
2. Patient information and mechanism of injury: Number of patients, age, sex, severity, and high-impact motor vehicle collision.
3. Clinical assessment and vital signs: Significant injuries, mental status, breathing difficulty, blood pressure, heart rate, and respiratory rate.
4. Care provided and resources requested: Trauma assessment, ongoing care, urgent trauma team evaluation, and acknowledgment of the two additional patients.
Safety consideration: In an actual ambulance, transporting two additional patients would require adequate approved seating and restraints, sufficient personnel, and compliance with local MCI transport protocols. Additional passengers must not compromise care of the critically injured patient.

Rubric
These answers will likely come in many forms. However, there is some standard, essential information that needs to be included in the answer. This includes:

-Identification of who is calling

-How many patients they have

-The triage color of their patients 

-How far out they are

The learner should not be penalized for extra information such as age, vital signs, injuries or mechanism. This is all important as well. However, if the answer is just a long ramble with an excessive amount of information, they should not receive full credit.

