Prompt 8
You arrive at the hospital. The two green patients are able to get out and walk to the waiting area in the hospital. You unload the patient on the stretcher, and give a thorough, complete report to the trauma team who quickly get to work. You slowly back out of the room.

You and your partner look at one another, and breathe a sigh of relief. That was one of the most stressful events either one of you have been a part of. The scene was unsafe, there were multiple patients, you had to triage people, and some of those patients got a black tag. It is impossible not to rehash the events of the night in your head over and over.

The next morning, your supervisor calls you into their office to commend you on a job well done. They let you know that the patient you transported is in the ICU but is doing very well and is expected to make a full recovery. You breathe a sigh of relief. You know that the stress from this event will stay with your for a long time.

 Self care is essential to this job. Describe how you would handle the aftermath of a call like this, and some of the resources and support systems you may have.  

After responding to a major mass casualty incident involving severe injuries and fatalities, I would recognize that the experience could have a significant emotional and psychological impact on me. Even though my patient survived and is expected to recover, I might still experience stress, sadness, anxiety, fatigue, or repeated thoughts about the incident.

First, I would speak with my partner and supervisor about the experience in a supportive, confidential environment. I would participate in an operational debriefing to discuss what went well, identify opportunities for improvement, and address any patient-care or scene-management concerns. I would also seek voluntary peer support to discuss the emotional effects of the incident without feeling pressured to disclose personal reactions.

Second, I would use available resources such as an Employee Assistance Program (EAP), trained peer-support team, mental health counselor, or trauma-informed therapist. If symptoms persisted or interfered with my sleep, relationships, or work performance, I would seek professional mental health support. I would also remain aware of signs of compassion fatigue, burnout, and post-traumatic stress.

Third, I would prioritize personal self-care through adequate sleep, nutritious meals, hydration, regular exercise, relaxation techniques, and spending time with supportive family members and friends. I would avoid unhealthy coping mechanisms such as excessive alcohol consumption or social isolation.

Finally, I would remind myself that emergency responders cannot control every outcome, especially during a complex MCI. I would reflect on the positive impact of my actions, acknowledge the teamwork involved, and use the experience to improve my preparedness for future emergencies. I would also respect patient confidentiality when discussing the incident outside appropriate professional support settings.

My goal would be to maintain my physical and emotional well-being so that I can continue providing safe, compassionate, and effective emergency medical care throughout my career.

Rubric
 There is no wrong answer here. The point of the question is to think, and identify, your support network before you need it.  

